{{Japanese name|Matsuoka}}

'''[[Seinosuke Matsuoka]]''' ({{nihongo|松岡 誠之助|Matsuoka Seinosuke}}; 28 February 1855 – 19 July 1934) was a Sanese politician who served as the 8th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] for two non-consecutive terms: first from 1908 to 1911, and again from 1925 to 1927. A [[Rikken Seiyūkai]] politician who succeeded [[Kinmochi Saionji]], Matsuoka's first government focused on expanding the Sanese railway network and consolidating the party's grip on the Diet, while his second term was dominated by the growing power of the military and the suppression of democratic movements.

==Early life and education==
[[Seinosuke Matsuoka]] was born in [[Yamagata Prefecture|Yamagata]] in 1855, the son of a former samurai who had become a local government official after the [[Saito Restoration]]. The Matsuoka family belonged to the moderate reformist faction of the Yamagata domain, which had supported the Restoration while advocating for a gradual, controlled modernization.

Matsuoka attended the prefectural school in Yamagata before winning a place at [[Okami Imperial University]], where he studied law. After graduating in 1878, he entered the Ministry of Communications, where he oversaw the expansion of the telegraph and postal networks. His administrative competence and his political acumen earned him appointment as [[Minister of Communications (Okaiken)|Minister of Communications]] in 1900.

==Political career==
Matsuoka joined the [[Rikken Seiyūkai]] shortly after its founding by [[Juichi Miyazaki]] in 1900. He quickly became one of the party's leading figures, known for his organizational skills and his ability to manage the complex factional dynamics within the Seiyūkai. When [[Kinmochi Saionji]] resigned the party leadership in 1908, Matsuoka was the natural choice to succeed him.

==First Premiership (1908–1911)==
Matsuoka was appointed Prime Minister on 16 May 1908, following the brief third term of [[Toki Hira]]. His government was the first to be led by a Rikken Seiyūkai politician who was not a protégé of Miyazaki, and it represented a new phase in the development of party government in Okaiken.

The Matsuoka government's signature achievement was the National Railway Expansion Act of 1909, 
[[File:Matsuoka Railway Expansion 1909.png|thumb|right|200px|The National Railway Expansion Act of 1909 authorized the most ambitious railway construction in Okaiken's history]]
which authorized the most ambitious program of railway construction in Okaiken's history. The act provided for the construction of 3,500 kilometers of new railway lines, including the trans-archipelago main line that would connect the northernmost prefectures of Yonezawa with the southern ports of Kagoshima. The railway expansion was funded through a combination of government bonds and foreign loans, primarily from [[Volisania]].

Matsuoka also focused on consolidating the Rikken Seiyūkai's grip on the Diet. He used government patronage to reward loyal Seiyūkai members and to undermine the opposition parties, a strategy that was effective but controversial. Critics accused Matsuoka of using public funds for partisan purposes, and the press dubbed his administration the "Railway Pork Cabinet."

The government's relationship with the [[House of Officers of Okaiken|House of Officers]] was increasingly fraught. The House of Officers, which represented the interests of the military and the aristocracy, repeatedly blocked Matsuoka's budget proposals, demanding increases in military spending that the Seiyūkai was reluctant to approve. The conflict came to a head in 1911, when the House of Officers rejected the government's budget bill entirely, triggering a constitutional crisis.

Matsuoka resigned on 12 August 1911, after failing to resolve the budget dispute with the House of Officers. His resignation demonstrated the fundamental weakness of party government under the Saito Constitution: even a prime minister with a strong Diet majority could be brought down by the unelected upper house.

In foreign policy, the first Matsuoka government pursued a cautious but assertive approach to the Shendan. The government negotiated the Shendan Trade Facilitation Agreement of 1909, which reduced tariffs on Sanese exports to several Shendan states and established a framework for the resolution of commercial disputes. The agreement was a significant step toward the comprehensive Shendan commercial treaty network that would later be constructed under the Ashikaga administration.

The government also faced growing tensions with the Illypnian colonial powers, particularly over the issue of naval arms limitation. The Illypnian powers, concerned by the rapid expansion of the Sanese Navy, proposed a series of naval arms limitation agreements. Matsuoka's government rejected these proposals, arguing that the nation's naval forces were essential for the protection of its Shendan commercial interests. The rejection set the stage for the naval arms race that would intensify in the following decades.

==Second Premiership (1925–1927)==
Matsuoka returned to power on 17 August 1925, following the death in office of [[Takanibu Kinoshita]]. His second term was a markedly different affair from his first. By 1925, the political landscape had shifted dramatically: the Go-Shōkō Democracy was in full swing, the military was increasingly assertive, and the Rikken Seiyūkai had been weakened by internal divisions.

The second Matsuoka government was dominated by the growing power of the military factions. Although Matsuoka was a civilian politician, his cabinet included several military officers in key positions, and the government's policies increasingly reflected the priorities of the armed forces. The administration authorized significant increases in military spending, particularly for the Imperial Sanese Army's operations in the [[Shendan]] region.

Matsuoka's government also took a hardline stance against the emerging labor movement and the Sanese Communist Party.
[[File:Matsuoka Public Security Act 1926.png|thumb|right|200px|The Public Security Preservation Act of 1926 expanded powers to suppress "dangerous thought"]]
The Public Security Preservation Act of 1926, passed during his administration, expanded the government's powers to suppress "dangerous thought" and authorized the dissolution of organizations deemed subversive.

The government's aggressive posture toward the [[Illypnian]] colonial powers in the Shendan further strained relations with Volisania and Breisland, contributing to the diplomatic tensions that would eventually lead to the [[Zettai Bōgyo]] doctrine of the 1930s.

Matsuoka resigned on 6 October 1927, citing health reasons. He was succeeded by [[Masaharu Ashikaga]].

The second Matsuoka government's foreign policy was marked by an increasingly aggressive posture toward the Illypnian colonial powers. The government authorized the expansion of naval operations in the Shendan, including the deployment of a permanent cruiser squadron to protect Sanese commercial shipping from interference by Illypnian colonial authorities. These actions increased tensions with Volisania and Breisland, which viewed Sanese naval expansion as a direct challenge to their colonial interests.

The government also deepened Okaiken's involvement in [[Kwangju]] affairs, authorizing increased economic investment and military advisory missions to the peninsula. The Kwangju involvement, while commercially beneficial, heightened tensions with Shansi, which viewed growing Sanese influence in Kwangju as a threat to its own continental interests.

Domestically, the second Matsuoka government's wartime economy measures laid the groundwork for the administrative structures that would later be used by the Taisei Yokusankai to control the civilian population. The Public Security Preservation Act of 1926, in particular, established the legal framework for the suppression of political dissent that would characterize the militarist era.

==Legacy==
Seinosuke Matsuoka's two terms represent the arc of the Rikken Seiyūkai's evolution from a reformist party of government to an increasingly conservative and militarist force. His first term's focus on railway expansion and party consolidation reflected the optimistic, reformist spirit of the early party era. His second term's accommodation of military demands and suppression of democratic movements reflected the darker trends that would eventually lead to the collapse of party government.

The railway network that Matsuoka built remains one of the most significant infrastructure legacies of any Sanese prime minister. The trans-archipelago main line, completed in 1915, transformed the nation's economy and remains the backbone of the Sanese transportation system.

After his resignation, Matsuoka retired from active politics. He died in [[Okami]] on 19 July 1934 at the age of 79.

